// Colourings of The Texts at a Glance, with the reading printed under each.
glance: {"nature":{"label":"1 · Nature","key":"mn","tok":"n","read":"The mixed view of the learner is the most common before modernity, in twelve of thirty-four texts, and only Xunzi and Augustine begin from a nature that is bad. The unfinished learner appears once before modernity, in Pico, and six times after it. In the modern row the good and mixed natures thin out, and a neutral nature, a set of capacities waiting to be formed, accounts for seventeen of the thirty-one texts."},"end":{"label":"2 · Where the end comes from","key":"es","tok":"e","read":"This is the colouring the governing question turns on. Thirty-three of the thirty-four texts before modernity place the end within the learner's nature; the exception is Pico, who leaves the choice to the learner. In the modern row the colours divide. Fifteen texts still place the end within the nature, five take it from the state or society, three from the market or from use, six leave it to the learner's choice, and two, Rousseau and Dewey, find it in growth itself."},"motive":{"label":"3 · Motive","key":"mo","tok":"m","read":"Longing for release or for God moves nine texts, all of them before modernity. Reward and use moves one pre-modern text, the Lotus Sutra, where the toy carts are only a means of getting the children out, and six modern ones. Will and striving moves two texts before modernity and twelve after it."},"scope":{"label":"4 · Scope","key":"sc","tok":"s","read":"Before modernity sixteen of thirty-four texts say that anyone can learn, nine grade learners by capacity and nine restrict learning by birth, sex or class. In the modern row twenty-two of thirty-one are open. The restriction that remains is by sex and class, in Locke, Rousseau and Sir Syed, and the grading is by capacity, from Macaulay's interpreters to Du Bois's talented tenth."},"authority":{"label":"5 · Authority","key":"au","tok":"a","read":"Before modernity the teacher is the most common source of authority, in seventeen of thirty-four texts, followed by tradition in nine; the state appears only with Aristotle's lawgiver and Fārābī's philosopher-ruler. In the modern row the self becomes the largest single source, in eleven of thirty-one texts, and the state rises to five."},"form":{"label":"Formation or transmission","key":"f","tok":"f","read":"Formation leads in every period. Holding both halves together is common before modernity, in twelve of thirty-four texts, and rare after it, in four of thirty-one. Transmission alone appears where a text teaches by method or by scriptural sentence (the Nyāya Sūtra, Śaṅkara), grades its learners (the Lotus Sutra, Ibn Rushd), or defends teaching as a real cause (Aquinas), and in modernity where an outside purpose takes over (Bacon, Macaulay, Sir Syed's college, Weber, human capital, Lyotard)."}},

